I'm joined today by Scott page who is  the leonid hurwicz collegiate professor  of complex systems at the university of  michigan also author of the recently  released book the diversity bonus and I  found the book very very interesting  Scott let's just start with trying to  define some terms here how do we measure  the bonus to businesses of more  diversity and what types of diversity  are we talking about so the way we  measure it is we just sort of ask how  much better does a team do when that  team is diverse versus a team that's  homogeneous and what I mean by diversity  here is I mean cognitive diversity  differences and how people think  differences in perspectives tools  training that sort of stuff that's going  to be related to identity diversity  sometimes very directly sometimes  indirectly but what really produces  these bonuses better predictions better  solutions is different ways of thinking  so one of the sort of ways to refute  arguments from some that there is sort  of an inherent caused by race difference  in IQ is that the differences in IQ  within races are far bigger than the  average differences between races what  about when it comes to cognitive  diversity can we make any kind of  analogy in the sense that does the do  the differences in cognitive diversity  vary more between different types of  diversity and identity groups or within  them I think that's a great question  that probably depends a little bit by  subject area so you'll hear people say  we value cognitive diversity and then  have all white man right so you can have  cognitive diversity among a group of all  white men but probably not as much now  if we're looking in an engineering  problem or a math problem the role that  identity plays in cognitive diversity  may be lower than if we're thinking  about health care policy or designing a  car or you know writing a screenplay or  something like that right the extent to  which our identities can affect how we  think about something he's gonna you  know obviously kind of bury by tap so  let's take one of the examples that you  said that this type of diversity would  be more noticeable when we talk about  designing a car for example or feel free  to pick whatever what  for example you think is sort of  illustrative of what you're talking  about  in what ways would we see that diversity  bone is reflected you know so again so  we've a woman's designing a car she's  gonna ask you know possibly where am I  gonna put my purse not to stereotype  right if someone who lives in the  suburbs for the larger families  designing a car they're gonna ask you  know does my dog fit in here writers dog  hair gonna get stick somebody could come  up with some seat that looks comfortable  but it could be like I'm never gonna get  the dog hair out of this right someone  who spends a lot of time you know  drinking coughing the car is gonna ask  you know where is my cupholders so the  thing is depending on you know who you  are and how you roll you're gonna you  know want different attributes in your  car you're gonna think about different  things is there a particular kind of  diversity that seems to be more helpful  than others in terms of race versus  gender versus economic background  whatever attribute we're talking about  so when you look at science and you ask  you know okay what you know  breakthroughs in science are  increasingly done by teams so if you  look at sort of most scientific papers  most academic papers are done by teams  the two things that really seem to sort  of move the needle or you know where  were you trained in so you don't get me  so what exactly is your knowledge base  and then the other thing that way seems  to matter is can like where did you  learn it so going to different schools  really produces a bonus so the three  sort of you know and then identities  probably the third thing so you know  what do you know where did you learn it  and who are you are kind of the three  big contributors to diversity bonuses  how could we separate causation from  correlation when we talk about something  like the business impact of diversity in  the sense that I could imagine that  companies that have a more diverse labor  force might do better because of some  other thing like for example more  dollars spent on recruitment and it's  not because of the diversity itself but  because of some third or other factors  how can we separate that out I think  that's actually you know difficult to do  in general and social science we  struggle you know separating out cause  agents and correlation one of the one of  the interesting things I've found over  the last decade have visited lots of  different organizations and you know for  profits nonprofits one thing you find is  that places that sort of embrace  identity diversity also embrace  cognitive diversity because they've got  a culture in which difference is  accepted so one of things that really  matters in terms of sort of getting  these diversity bonuses if is someone  being willing to share a novel idea so  when we say hey have we thought about  this so the episode of a culture of fear  or a culture in which everybody's forced  to sort of like you know just repeat the  company line you're not gonna get these  bonuses and so you're right it could be  the case that the presence of identity  diversity is really just sort of a  signal of the fact that this is a place  where people feel comfortable and the  people feel comfortable they're more  likely to share ideas the other thing  that's interesting about identity  diversity and this is work by Katherine  Phillips who also contributed to this  book I wrote is that if I'm in an  identity diverse group I often feel more  compelled to fully explain myself and  when I do that I might either I might or  someone else might see a flaw in my  logic so if you bunch of all white male  economists in a room they don't like you  sort of like Oh mark it's work people  are rational right throw in a woman  psychologist and then they're kind of  like well let me explain why people are  rational when as they walk through that  they might say yeah maybe this is an  emotional purchase or maybe they're not  that rational or maybe they're thinking  about other things so the mere presence  of diversity can cause you to sort of  think differently and get better answers  so so far we've been talking mostly  about what would be on the sort of pro  side of workplace diversity what about  or how do we deal with the  counter-arguments like for example  without diversity you eliminate  diversity based discrimination as  something that can be a weight on the  day-to-day functioning of a business or  team or you eliminate some degree of  cultural incongruity or whatever how can  we measure the sort of net effect let's  grant all of the pros that you've  identified how do we then back out and  consider the possible cons of diversity  that some might point to oh so the cons  are there so if you want someone to say  oh all diversity all the time like I'm  not your guy but here's how I think  about this is that on a day to day sort  of rolling basis any organization is  gonna have some complex problems and  some easy problems  I'm the easier problems  you're kind of better off going  homogeneity because there's not gonna be  any diversity bonus so in the book I  talk a lot about kind of like chopping  down trees if we're chopping down trees  there's no diversity bonus right but if  we're curing cancer there's a big  diversity bonus so what you want to do  is you kind of want to do this is what I  call sort of a complexity diversity  triage when the problem comes in the  door you got to decide is this a  high-value complex problem and if it is  you throw the diversity at it right and  you hope the benefits outweigh the cost  if it's simple you just give it to  somebody and let them handle it but if  you don't have that diversity in house  if you haven't built a culture if you  haven't built sort of protocols where  you can get those diversity bonuses then  you're gonna screw up the high-value  complex problems and you're gonna go  under you can see this one the places  where you see this go into an emergency  room if you like twisted your ankle  somebody looks at you and they say  simple may hand you off to someone who  tapes it up and they send you home if  you're presenting in some really  complicated confusing way they're gonna  walk you past a whole bunch of different  doctors then a group of doctors will  meet then they'll come back and look at  you and ask a bunch of questions and you  get a very sophisticated complex  diagnosis it comes at a higher cost  absolutely but the point is it's a more  important problem like you're not gonna  die from a twisted ankle and so this is  I view this really is about diversity as  a strategic asset so if you have the  culture and the people in house to apply  cognitive diversity right then the  challenge is identifying those tasks  where you use it and let's say you  haven't yet built this diverse team what  are ways to do that effectively I mean  we've I've done some research into  so-called diversity policies there's  been this recent one with this former  Google engineer James the more he  criticized the diversity policy it  became his whole thing I don't need you  to comment on that necessarily unless  you want to but there is a longitudinal  study of 700 US companies which found  that diversity training programs and  policies did not really seem to have a  net positive effect specifically when it  came to the representation of that one  looked at black women for example so  talked a little bit about diversity  policies and what are effective ways to  increase diversity okay so if you look  at the societal level right there's just  gonna be there's gonna be some  percentage of people in each of these  identity categories that's graduated  from college  is gonna be sort of on-boarded into  corporate America on-boarded into sort  of government agencies and it's not that  maybe sort of in some sense kind of like  zero-sum in a way right people are gonna  go work one place maybe not another  place but if you look across all  industries you know you they it's not  necessarily possible that they could all  work unless you sort of create more  people in one of these categories I  think though what you really want to ask  is are these diverse teams functioning  well and producing better you know sort  of producing pragmatic benefits within  these organizations and one of the  things that you find and this is sort of  you know gripped by David Thomas and  Robin Ely at Harvard and Jeff polls are  at Harvard beta Maddox at Cornell is  that if people think that the diversity  is there because it's the right thing to  do  you're not gonna see bonuses but you're  not gonna see big benefits from it but  if people think the diversity is there  because it gives you benefits you're  likely to see the benefit because you  kind of get what you're asking for right  if you're thinking like well you look  different than me you were trained  different with me you're from a  different place you can probably help me  if that's how I approach you  odds are we may get something positive  from that but if instead of my glue  David you're kind of different for me  you look different from me okay I'll be  nice to you because I'm inclusive we're  not likely to see anything really  exciting yet that makes sense but who is  it that would have one or the other of  those perspectives is it managers is it  people in HR deciding on hiring is it  colleagues like who is it that would  have the possibility of either relating  to diversity as something we're supposed  to do or something that genuinely  improves the team I think this is what  it really comes from the top from  leadership from the top and corporate  cultures I think you had a time when  most when a lot of the workforce that  capped the workforce in the 1960 was  engaged in sort of you know physical  labor manufacturing sort of jobs jobs  that didn't produce these bonuses that  affirmative action programs diversity  programs were framed in terms of  inclusiveness like let's be inclusive  let's not discriminate I think when you  move to the knowledge economy if you go  to Ford if you go to Google if you go to  Northrop Grumman right if you go to  Bloomberg you're gonna see leadership  talking about the necessity having  cognitively diverse people working on  hard problems so Astro teller at Google  X which is kind of like you know  stretch out for an innovative place you  know he sounds like an HR diversity  person when he talks about human capital  he's like we need people who think  differently we need people with diverse  perspective and so I think that it  really comes from the top but then it  also has to be you know sort of carried  out within the protocols of the firm the  book is the diversity bonus we've been  speaking with the book's author  University of Michigan professor Scott  page Scott very interesting and I really  appreciate you talking to us about it  thanks a lot it's great opportunity  thank you

